A client that declares package foo of its own can name foo.Open and extend
it, and this app does. mima counts foo.Open as closed, because a client
outside foo cannot name it, so it reports nothing and the app dies.

Arguably it should report: the client is legal Scala. mima does not protect
that client anywhere else either, see
package-private-method-changes-same-package-app-ok, where a changed
private[foo] method goes unreported and the same app dies. Reporting here
and not there would be the worse inconsistency, so this waits for a
decision about qualified private as a whole.
